{"success": true, "data": {"stdout": "{"id": 0, "depth": 0, "text": "Anarchism is a political philosophy and movement that is skeptical of all justifications for authority and seeks to abolish the institutions it claims maintain unnecessary coercion and hierarchy, typically including nation-states, and capitalism. Anarchism advocates for the replacement of the state with stateless societies and voluntary free associations. As a historically left-wing movement, this reading of anarchism is placed on the farthest left of the political spectrum, usually described as the libertarian wing of the socialist movement (libertarian socialism).\n\nHumans have lived in societies without formal hierarchies long before the establishment of states, realms, or empires. With the rise of organised hierarchical bodies, scepticism toward authority also rose. Although traces of anarchist ideas are found all throughout history, modern anarchism emerged from the Enlightenment. During the latter half of the 19th and the first decades of the 20th century, the anarchist movement flourished in most parts of the world and had a significant role in workers' struggles for emancipation. Various anarchist schools of thought formed during this period. Anarchists have taken part in several revolutions, most notably in the Paris Commune, the Russian Civil War and the Spanish Civil War, whose end marked the end of the classical era of anarchism. In the last", "seed_id": 0, "token_count": 256, "model": "qwen3-0.6b-instruct"}
{"id": 1, "depth": 1, "text": "In the 20th century, the anarchist movement continued to grow, influencing movements for social justice and equality. Today, anarchism remains a relevant and dynamic political thought, advocating for a society where power is distributed among individuals and where collective action is the foundation of human progress.", "seed_id": 0, "token_count": 56, "model": "qwen3-0.6b-instruct"}
{"id": 2, "depth": 2, "text": "Anarchism continues to inspire new generations, fostering a culture of self-sufficiency and community-driven change.", "seed_id": 0, "token_count": 22, "model": "qwen3-0.6b-instruct"}
", "stderr": "", "return_code": 0}, "error": null, "metadata": {"elapsed_seconds": 0.35}}

{"success": true, "data": {"stdout": "{"id": 0, "depth": 0, "text": "Anarchism is a political philosophy and movement that is skeptical of all justifications for authority and seeks to abolish the institutions it claims maintain unnecessary coercion and hierarchy, typically including nation-states, and capitalism. Anarchism advocates for the replacement of the state with stateless societies and voluntary free associations. As a historically left-wing movement, this reading of anarchism is placed on the farthest left of the political spectrum, usually described as the libertarian wing of the socialist movement (libertarian socialism).\n\nHumans have lived in societies without formal hierarchies long before the establishment of states, realms, or empires. With the rise of organised hierarchical bodies, scepticism toward authority also rose. Although traces of anarchist ideas are found all throughout history, modern anarchism emerged from the Enlightenment. During the latter half of the 19th and the first decades of the 20th century, the anarchist movement flourished in most parts of the world and had a significant role in workers' struggles for emancipation. Various anarchist schools of thought formed during this period. Anarchists have taken part in several revolutions, most notably in the Paris Commune, the Russian Civil War and the Spanish Civil War, whose end marked the end of the classical era of anarchism. In the last", "seed_id": 0, "token_count": 256, "model": "qwen3-1.7b-instruct"}
{"id": 1, "depth": 1, "text": "decades of the 20th century, the anarchist movement experienced a decline, though it continued to influence political and social thought. The rise of totalitarian regimes and the expansion of state power in the 20th century led to a significant reduction in the visibility and influence of anarchist ideas. However, the legacy of anarchism persists in contemporary movements advocating for individual freedom, social justice, and the abolition of coercive institutions.", "seed_id": 0, "token_count": 86, "model": "qwen3-1.7b-instruct"}
{"id": 2, "depth": 2, "text": "This legacy is evident in modern movements that draw inspiration from anarchist principles, such as the global justice movement, the rise of anti-capitalist and anti-authoritarian ideologies, and the ongoing struggle for democratic governance and human rights. While the formal anarchist movement has waned, its core ideas continue to resonate in discussions about decentralization, autonomy, and the rejection of systemic oppression.", "seed_id": 0, "token_count": 74, "model": "qwen3-1.7b-instruct"}
", "stderr": "", "return_code": 0}, "error": null, "metadata": {"elapsed_seconds": 0.9}}

